File System Hierarchy:
/ - top level root directory
bin - binary directory contains essential directories to execute tasks such as cp, mkdir etc required for system functioning
boot - contains data/files required for boot process, has linux, ram, boot loaded conf files etc.
dev - has device files, eg: usb, printers, harddisks.
etc - extra conf files, has params for services, apps etc, eg: user auth files
home -  user's home directories stored here and user files stored here
lib  - all library files related to bin, sbin needed at runtime
lib64 - all library files related to bin, sbin needed at runtime
local  - all local data
media  - has external media such as cd' usb etc , for mount purposes
mnt - used for mounting temp file systems
opt - all optional packages, softwares stored here
proc  - has all processes info
root  - root user's home directory
run  - stores volatile runtime data in your RAM
sbin  - system binaries directory contains essential files/directories used by sys admins to execute tasks which require admin privileges
srv  - stores site-specific data that is served by the system to external network clients
sys - mount point for sysfs, a virtual filesystem created by the kernel to expose system hardware, device drivers, and kernel subsystems to userspace
tmp  - all temp files/directories stored here
usr - all user related files/directories stored here
var - variables files that change during system operations, eg: log files


